<header class="hero"><div><div class="kicker">Workshop scenario · two connected services</div><h1>Break in.<br><em>Follow the trail.</em></h1><p class="lead">A PHP web server fronts an internal ledger. The wallet record is one network hop away, but the vault is invisible from your browser. Your job is to find a path through the PHP server.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#mission">View the mission ↓</a><a class="button alt" href="#network">Inspect the network</a></div></div><div class="vault-art" role="img" aria-label="Illustration of a sealed vault"><div class="lock">▣</div><div class="artlabel">LEDGER VAULT / ACCESS RESTRICTED</div></div></header>
<section id="mission" class="section"><div class="kicker">Mission briefing</div><h2>Recover the vault proof.</h2><p>The public web server is your entry point. Inside its environment are operational clues about a separate ledger service. Reach that service, inspect the account records, and recover the proof from the wallet record.</p><div class="cards"><div class="card"><div class="num">01 / ENTRY</div><h3>Inspect the PHP server</h3><p>Identify the PHP version and its CLI server workers behind nginx. The issue is in PHP itself, before any application logic is required.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">02 / DISCOVERY</div><h3>Trace the internal service</h3><p>A second container sits behind the web tier. Its address and access details are discoverable from the PHP environment.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">03 / OBJECTIVE</div><h3>Open the ledger</h3><p>The vault contains account records and a wallet entry. The final proof has the form <code>PHP2SHELL{...}</code>.</p></div></div></section>
<section id="network" class="section"><div class="kicker">Environment map</div><h2>One public door. One private vault.</h2><div class="topology" aria-label="Browser to PHP server to internal vault"><div class="node"><div class="icon">◉</div><strong>Your browser</strong><small>localhost:8080</small></div><div class="connector"></div><div class="node"><div class="icon">⌘</div><strong>PHP web server</strong><small>nginx → PHP <?= e($version) ?></small></div><div class="connector"></div><div class="node private"><div class="icon">▣</div><strong>Ledger vault</strong><small>Private Docker network · port 9000</small></div></div><div class="facts"><div class="fact">PHP <b><?= e($version) ?></b></div><div class="fact">SAPI <b><?= e($sapi) ?></b></div><div class="fact">Memory limit <b><?= e((string) ini_get('memory_limit')) ?></b></div><div class="fact">Vault <b>internal only</b></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="kicker">What you are studying</div><h2>Two PHP vulnerability groups.</h2><div class="cards"><div class="card"><div class="num">CVE-2026-109618</div><h3>OOM corruption</h3><p>Out-of-memory errors can interrupt initialization and leave request state invalid when shutdown processes it. Study this as a related mechanism.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">CVE-2026-109619</div><h3>Stale global state</h3><p>Request-owned session state can survive in a worker and be consumed by a later upload-progress request. This is the lifecycle path used in the lab.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="num">WORKSHOP FOCUS</div><h3>Follow the lifecycle</h3><p>Observe how request startup, failure handling, worker reuse, and shutdown affect the reachable state.</p></div></div></section>
